# =============================================================================
# .gitignore — the list of files Git should pretend don't exist
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES
# Anything matching a pattern below will never be committed, never be pushed,
# and won't clutter up `git status`. Git simply ignores it.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS
# Two reasons — one boring, one serious.
#   1. Boring: keeps junk out (OS clutter, editor settings, build output).
#   2. Serious: keeps SECRETS out. Once a password is pushed to GitHub, treat
#      it as public forever. Deleting the file later does NOT remove it from
#      the repo's history — anyone can still dig it out of old commits.
#
# THE RULE TO REMEMBER
# It is easy to add a file to Git. It is genuinely hard to remove one properly.
# So: get .gitignore right BEFORE your first commit, not after.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SYNTAX GOTCHA — read this, it catches everyone
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Git does NOT support comments at the end of a pattern line. This is broken:
#
#     .DS_Store        # macOS junk file      <-- WRONG
#
# Git reads that whole line as one filename, and looks for a file literally
# called ".DS_Store        # macOS junk file". The real .DS_Store is NOT
# ignored, and nothing warns you. Always put comments on their own line.
#
# Check whether a pattern actually works:
#     git check-ignore -v .env
# Prints the matching rule if it's ignored, prints nothing if it isn't.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. SECRETS AND CREDENTIALS          *** MOST IMPORTANT SECTION ***
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: always, in every project, from the very first commit.
# WHY: these files hold passwords, API keys and tokens. Committing one can
#      expose a client database, run up a cloud bill, or leak personal data.
#      Bots actively scan public GitHub for leaked keys — within minutes.
#
# Note the pattern: `.env` is ignored, but `.env.example` is NOT (it holds
# only fake placeholder values). That's how your teammates learn which
# variables they need without you ever sending them a real password.

.env
.env.local
.env.development
.env.production
.env.*.local

# Private keys and certificates — these prove you are you. Never share them.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Credential files that tools sometimes generate for you
config/secrets.json
credentials.json
service-account.json
*.credentials


# =============================================================================
# 2. OPERATING SYSTEM FILES
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: whenever your team uses more than one type of computer.
# WHY: your OS quietly creates metadata files (window positions, thumbnails)
#      inside your folders. They're useless to everyone else and cause noisy
#      "changes" in every pull request. Not a security risk — just mess.

# macOS
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*

# Windows
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# Linux
*~
.directory


# =============================================================================
# 3. EDITOR AND IDE SETTINGS
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: when teammates use different editors, or different setups.
# WHY: these are YOUR personal preferences (theme, window layout, cursor
#      position). Committing them means fighting over each other's settings.
#
# Judgement call: some teams DO commit .vscode/ deliberately, to share agreed
# formatting rules. Either is defensible — just decide as a team.

.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*.sublime-workspace
.project


# =============================================================================
# 4. DEPENDENCIES
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: any project that installs external packages.
# WHY: dependency folders are enormous (often tens of thousands of files) and
#      fully reproducible — anyone can regenerate them from the lockfile with
#      one command. Committing them bloats the repo for no benefit.
#
# This particular project has no dependencies at all. These lines are here as
# a teaching example, since you'll see them in nearly every real repo.

node_modules/
bower_components/
vendor/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
venv/
.venv/


# =============================================================================
# 5. BUILD OUTPUT AND GENERATED FILES
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: projects with a build step (most modern web apps).
# WHY: build output is *derived* from your source code. Commit the recipe, not
#      the cake — otherwise every build creates a huge confusing diff.
#
# This project has no build step: the .html files you edit are exactly what
# the browser loads. That's unusual, and deliberate — one less thing to learn.

dist/
build/
out/
*.bundle.js
*.min.js
.cache/


# =============================================================================
# 6. LOGS AND TEMPORARY FILES
# =============================================================================
# WHEN this applies: basically always.
# WHY: logs are generated at runtime, grow forever, and can accidentally
#      contain sensitive data (user emails, tokens, internal URLs). Worth a
#      second thought before sharing one, even in a bug report.

*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log
logs/
*.tmp
.temp/


# =============================================================================
# WANT TO GO FURTHER?
# =============================================================================
# GitHub maintains ready-made .gitignore files for every language:
#   https://github.com/github/gitignore
#
# When you start a new repo on GitHub, it offers to add the right one for you.
# Take it — it's a better starting point than writing this from scratch.
# =============================================================================
